In this lesson we'll look at four ways statisticians collect data, and why the choice matters. First up is a census, which means you measure absolutely everyone in the population, like a national census does. The upside is you get exact answers, no uncertainty. The downside is it's slow and really expensive, so it only makes sense for huge, official counts. Next is sampling, where you measure a smaller group and use that to infer things about everyone else. It's cheaper and quicker, but there's always a bit of uncertainty, because your sample might not perfectly mirror the whole population, and that's called sampling variation. Then we have experiments, where you actually give some people a treatment and compare them to a control group who don't get it. When it's set up well, with random allocation, you can actually pin down cause and effect, not just association, though sometimes it's unethical to run one. And finally, observation, where you just watch and record what people do without interfering. It's easy, but it can only show that two things are linked, never that one caused the other. Oh, and keep an eye out for bias, which can sneak into any of these methods. Now let's see it in action.